  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "The return comes from occupancy and rent, not from cutting expenses",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Mesa Ridge is [[sf]] rentable square feet running at [[occ_now]] occupancy. The repositioning takes occupancy to [[occ_plan]] and base rent from [[rent_now]] to [[rent_plan]] per square foot, lifting NOI per square foot from [[noipsf_now]] to [[noipsf_plan]].",
        "Operating expense per square foot barely moves, from [[opex_now]] to [[opex_plan]]. That is deliberate. The plan does not assume a management or maintenance efficiency we have not already demonstrated at comparable assets, so none of the return depends on cost discipline that has yet to be proven here.",
        "Both leasing profiles are held at their current market assumptions; Exhibit B shows them side by side so the committee can see exactly which inputs differ between the warehouse and flex units."
      ],
      "bullets": [
        "Repairs and maintenance falls from [[rm_y1]] in Year 1 to [[rm_y3]] as deferred work clears \u2014 a consequence of the capital spend, not an assumed saving.",
        "Property taxes are held at [[tax_y1]] in Year 1 with a reassessment assumed in Year 2; the assessor's schedule, not our estimate, drives that line."
      ],
      "exhibits": [
        "A",
        "B"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Operating statement and the return on capital",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Total revenue moves from [[rev_y1]] in Year 1 to [[rev_y3]] at stabilization, against NOI of [[noi_y1]] in the first year. Capital of [[capital]] returns in [[payback]] months, producing a five-year IRR of [[irr]] and a going-in cap rate of [[cap]].",
        "Debt service coverage stabilizes at [[dscr]], which clears the lender covenant with room. The reimbursement line carries the most estimation risk, since it depends on the lease structures signed during lease-up rather than on anything currently in place."
      ],
      "exhibits": [
        "C"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The downside still services debt",
      "paragraphs": [
        "On the downside case, stabilized NOI falls to [[down_noi]] and payback extends to [[down_pb]] months. Coverage holds above the covenant at that level, so the downside is a slower return rather than a default risk. The upside case reaches [[up_noi]].",
        "The decision point is lease-up pace. If signed occupancy has not cleared the midpoint between current and stabilized by the end of month nine, the correct response is to hold rents and extend the lease-up window, not to discount to fill space.",
        "Exhibit E walks a single representative lease month by month, so the shape of the leasing-cost drag in the commencement month is visible rather than buried in an annual total."
      ],
      "exhibits": [
        "D",
        "E"
      ]
    }
  ],
